{"title":"Philosophy","timeMinutes":10,"questions":[{"text":"What Descartes said about the single certain truth?","image":"","options":["Clarity of thought","Methodological scepticism","Deliberate doubt","Distinctness in idea"],"correctIdx":2},{"text":"According to Spinoza, God is regarded as what?","image":"","options":["Causa Sui","Everything","Nothing","Dual"],"correctIdx":0},{"text":"What does Kant mean by phenomenal reality?","image":"","options":["World as phenomena","World as we experience it","World as reality","World in space and time"],"correctIdx":1},{"text":"St. Augustine believes that the real problem is moral evil and it is because of . . . . . . . .","image":"","options":["Religion","Ethinicity","Caste","Class"],"correctIdx":0},{"text":"In vedic philosophy\n\"The subject-object duality is transcended in the deep sleep stage\" belong to which stage?","image":"","options":["Taijasa","Turiya","Prajna","Vishwa"],"correctIdx":2},{"text":"What is the term used for the limitation of freedom by Sartre?","image":"","options":["Epoche","Dread","Facticity","Phenomenology"],"correctIdx":2},{"text":"According to Hobbes, what is the state of nature of the live in the social contract?","image":"","options":["Better than life in his own time","Free and noble","Poor, solitary, nasty, brutish and short","Difficult but honesty"],"correctIdx":2},{"text":"Which philosophical school teaches that pleasure and the absence of pain are central to a good life?","image":"","options":["Stoicism","Existentialism","Nihilism","Epicureanism"],"correctIdx":3},{"text":"What is the main philosophical challenge presented by the problem of evil?","image":"","options":["It proves that suffering has no psychological explanation.","It questions how an all-powerful, all-knowing, and perfectly good God can coexist with evil.","It demonstrates that morality is necessarily subjective.","It establishes that all religions teach the same thing."],"correctIdx":1},{"text":"What is the central philosophical implication of Nagarjuna's concept of śūnyatā (emptiness)?","image":"","options":["Nothing exists in any sense whatsoever.","The external world is merely a dream created by one mind.","Things lack intrinsic, independent existence and arise dependently.","Only spiritual entities are real."],"correctIdx":2},{"text":"According to Schopenhauer, what fundamentally drives existence?","image":"","options":["Rational self-interest","Divine providence","The pursuit of objective truth","A blind, incessant will underlying the world of appearances"],"correctIdx":3},{"text":"According to David Hume, what is the fundamental problem with inductive reasoning?","image":"","options":["It contradicts mathematical logic.","It assumes that human beings cannot perceive reality.","It assumes that the future will resemble the past without a non-circular justification.","It proves that causation does not exist."],"correctIdx":2},{"text":"Which philosopher is most closely associated with the concept of bad faith (mauvaise foi)?","image":"","options":["Albert Camus","Søren Kierkegaard","Martin Heidegger","Jean-Paul Sartre"],"correctIdx":3},{"text":"What does the lion symbolize in the three metamorphoses of Nietzsche?","image":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","options":["Blind obedience to tradition","Complete rejection of life","The struggle for freedom against established values and commands","The desire to return to childhood"],"correctIdx":2},{"text":"Which philosophical problem is particularly prominent in Kafka's The Trial?","image":"","options":["Whether numbers exist independently of human minds","Whether animals possess language","How people experience guilt, powerlessness, and authority when the rules governing their lives remain inaccessible","Whether beauty can be measured mathematically"],"correctIdx":2}]}